:"charts","grp":"1001 Albums","list_year":null,"rank":199,"year":1970,"note":null,"total":999,"scaruffi":null},{"id":3080531282495,"name":"AOTY Must Hear 1970s","src":"musthear","grp":null,"list_year":null,"rank":243,"year":null,"note":null,"total":256,"scaruffi":null},{"id":198433558933938,"name":"This album is the BEST in its GENRE @Frenchblues","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":537,"year":null,"note":null,"total":730,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Don Gallucci, former keyboard player for The Kingsmen, later went on to produce The Stooges' album \"Fun House.\" \"The loot she went on to produce fun house for the stooge's\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 106:\"Louie Louie\" by the Kingsmen","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S3agGJsxjpE"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Named after their living quarters in Detroit, this album was recorded in Los Angeles. The recording process was characterized by extensive drug use, including the band's first experience with cocaine. The album was recorded largely live off the floor with minimal overdubs to capture their raw live sound, with its seven songs recorded in sequence like a live gig. Released in July 1970, *Fun House* is retrospectively considered the best representation of The Stooges' sound, though at the time it was savaged by critics and commercially unsuccessful. \"That whole record was recorded live off the floor.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"What's The Big Deal About The Stooges?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w-rihv544I8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"One of three major label albums recorded between August 1969 and May 1973. During this period, the band's lineup shifted almost monthly. \"The stooages recorded three major label albums between August 1969 and May 1973 with a line up shifting almost monthly.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"What's The Big Deal About Iggy Pop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OxctiPr0l-8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"This second album pushed The Stooges' sound to its extreme, becoming even rawer and dirtier. It was heavily influenced and contaminated by free jazz, improvisation, and pure chaos. Initially, critics struggled to understand it, with Melody Maker calling it the \"worst album of the year,\" though decades later, it would be acclaimed as one of the best rock albums in history, illustrating how perception can evolve over time. \"Pero fue con su segundo disco, Fan House, donde la banda llevó su sonido al extremo.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Central Sonora","episode":"El punk existía antes de Sex Pistols (y era algo salvaje)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ilXpBujkWZE"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Second Album","body":"Named after their living quarters in Detroit, this album was recorded in Los Angeles. The recording process was characterized by extensive drug use, including the band's first experience with cocaine. The album was recorded largely live off the floor with minimal overdubs to capture their raw live sound, with its seven songs recorded in sequence like a live gig. Released in July 1970, *Fun House* is retrospectively considered the best representation of The Stooges' sound, though at the time it was savaged by critics and commercially unsuccessful. \"That whole record was recorded live off the floor.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"What's The Big Deal About The Stooges?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w-rihv544I8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Second Studio Album (example)","body":"One of three major label albums recorded between August 1969 and May 1973. During this period, the band's lineup shifted almost monthly. \"The stooages recorded three major label albums between August 1969 and May 1973 with a line up shifting almost monthly.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"What's The Big Deal About Iggy Pop","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OxctiPr0l-8"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":57226208215724,"artist":"The Stooges","artist_id":280372946476675,"title":"Fun House","year":1970,"ess":0,"yt":"B9Q5luXFkAI","kind":null,"book":{"id":7,"title":"Postpunk. Romper todo y empezar de nuevo","author":"Simon Reynolds","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Postpunk/"},"scene":"The Velvet Underground y sus herederos","scene_id":187314516147781,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Postpunk/#velvet","chapters":["Prólogo: La revolución inconclusa"],"pages":[]},{"id":57226208215724,"artist":"Stooges","artist_id":280372946476675,"title":"Fun House","year":1970,"ess":1,"yt":"B9Q5luXFkAI","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Detroit: MC5 y Stooges","scene_id":65355204336384,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#detroit","chapters":["Sección 3.8"],"pages":[[1,73,null]]}],"others":[{"id":134313687194497,"type":"album","name":"The Stooges","year":1969,"weight":54},{"id":264579915859127,"type":"album","name":"Raw Power","year":1973,"weight":36},{"id":183516578594534,"type":"song","name":"I Wanna Be Your Dog","year":1969,"weight":13},{"id":140753250316197,"type":"song","name":"1969","year":1969,"weight":13},{"id":34919237171096,"type":"song","name":"No Fun","year":1969,"weight":13},{"id":56728425595313,"type":"song","name":"Search and Destroy","year":1973,"weight":10},{"id":3317795484008,"type":"album","name":"Metallic KO","year":1974,"weight":10},{"id":281080302908671,"type":"song","name":"Louie Louie (Cover)","year":null,"weight":7},{"id":232748417479620,"type":"song","name":"Louie Louie","year":1974,"weight":7},{"id":14815994